          id: "ch22-polymorphism",
          title: "Polymorphism",
          whyItMatters: "Polymorphism lets different classes respond to the same method in their own way.",
          realWorldAnalogy: "Polymorphism is like different tools - a hammer and screwdriver both 'fasten' but differently.",
          content: `Method Overriding

class Shape:
    def area(self):
        pass

class Circle(Shape):
    def area(self):
        return 3.14 * self.radius ** 2

class Square(Shape):
    def area(self):
        return self.side ** 2

Polymorphic Function

def print_area(shape):
    print(shape.area())`,
          codeExamples: [
            {
              id: "ch22-polymorphism",
              title: "Polymorphism Example",
              description: "Same method, different behaviors",
              code: { python: "class Animal:\n    def speak(self):\n        pass\n\nclass Dog(Animal):\n    def speak(self):\n        return \"Woof\"\n\nclass Cat(Animal):\n    def speak(self):\n        return \"Meow\"\n\nfor animal in [Dog(), Cat()]:\n    print(animal.speak())" },
              explanation: "Polymorphism allows different behaviors through same interface."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      exercises: [
