          whyItMatters: "ABCs define interfaces that subclasses must implement.",
          realWorldAnalogy: "ABCs are like contracts - subclasses must fulfill the agreement.",
          content: `Creating ABCs

from abc import ABC, abstractmethod

class Shape(ABC):
    @abstractmethod
    def area(self):
        pass
    
    @abstractmethod
    def perimeter(self):
        pass

Subclasses Must Implement

class Circle(Shape):
    def area(self):
        return 3.14 * self.radius ** 2`,
          codeExamples: [
            {
              id: "ch27-abc",
              title: "Abstract Base Classes",
              description: "Defining interfaces",
              code: { python: "from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n\nclass Animal(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    def speak(self):\n        pass\n\nclass Dog(Animal):\n    def speak(self):\n        return \"Woof!\"\n\n# Cannot instantiate Animal directly\n# Must implement abstract methods in subclasses" },
              explanation: "ABCs enforce method implementation in subclasses."
            }
          ]
        }
